<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=quantum-mechanics">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Science and discovery</p><h1 class="enc-title">Quantum mechanics</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1900">1900</a>–<a href="/years/1927">1927</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Quantum physics, Quantum theory</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Quantum mechanics is the physical theory that describes the behaviour of matter and light at and below the scale of atoms; it arose from early theories such as Max Planck's hypothesis of 1900 that energy is radiated and absorbed in discrete &quot;quanta&quot;. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-einstein">Albert Einstein</a> used the idea in 1905 to explain the photoelectric effect, and modern quantum mechanics was born in 1925, when Werner Heisenberg, Max Born and Pascual Jordan developed matrix mechanics and Erwin Schrödinger invented wave mechanics. Its applications include the laser, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transistor">transistor</a> and magnetic resonance imaging.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/quantum-mechanics.jpg" alt="Quantum mechanics" width="320" height="232" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Benjamin Couprie · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Solvay_conference_1927.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>1900</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd>Max Planck · Werner Heisenberg · Erwin Schrödinger</dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>Planck’s quantum hypothesis of 1900 began it; modern quantum mechanics was formulated in 1925–1926.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Led to</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/nuclear-fission">Nuclear fission<span>1938</span></a><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/transistor">Transistor<span>1947</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=quantum-mechanics">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
